WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:45.000
It's 5 o'clock and your workday is technically over, but you tell yourself you'll just answer one more email. Before you know it, two hours have passed and you're still at your desk. Or you're supposed to be on vacation, but you can't resist reading work emails and responding every time your phone pings with a message from a colleague. In our always-connected world, it's easier than ever to let work encroach on every hour of the day. But psychologists who study workaholism say that compulsive overwork is detrimental. to our physical and mental health, and perhaps surprisingly, even to our careers. Today we're going to talk to a psychologist who studies workaholism about what it is and how to combat it. What's the difference between being a workaholic and just being a hard worker who's

2
00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:36.000
invested in her job? Is workaholism an addiction? How does it affect people's physical and mental health and their relationships? What can you do if you recognize workaholic tendencies in yourself or in someone you care about, and what can organizations and employers do to encourage a healthy relationship with work among their employees? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. My guest today is Dr. Melissa Clark, a professor of industrial organizational psychology at the University of Georgia, where she directs the Healthy Work Lab. She's a leading expert on workaholism overwork and employee well-being. Her research has been published in top academic journals

3
00:01:36.000 --> 00:02:24.000
and featured in publications including Time, The New York Times, The Atlantic, and U.S. News and World Report. Her 2024 book, Never Not Working, Why the Always On Culture is Bad for Business and How to Fix It, was recognized as a top management book by People Management, Management Today, and The Next Big Idea club. She also recently spoke with APA's magazine Monitor on Psychology for an article on workaholism and work addiction. Dr. Clark, thank you for joining me. Thank you so much for having me. I've seen workaholism go by a few different names including work addiction and compulsive overwork. What term do you use in your research and how is this situation different from just working really hard or being very dedicated to one's job? I use the term workaholism to be on.

4
00:02:24.000 --> 00:03:08.000
honest, I don't love the term work addiction, but it is what it is at this point. It's what is coined. It's not a formal addiction in the DSM, and I'm also not a clinical psychologist, which is partly why I don't use the work addiction term specifically. But workaholism is multifaceted, and a lot of people don't realize that. They think, okay, it's just someone who works all the time. But those examples that you pointed out really touch on how nuanced it is. And the way I study it and measure it, is actually with four different components. There's a motivational piece where people feel like they ought to be working all the time. You probably have heard of a concept called intrinsic motivation, which is working because you love to work.

5
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Workaholism is driven by introjected motivation. It's a concept that psychologists know. It's working because you feel like you ought to. It's partially internalized values of society and, you know, things that you learn growing up. but it's not truly of your own volition. So it's this kind of controlled motivation. But I just describe it as pit in my stomach, you know, feeling like I ought to be working.

6
00:03:33.000 --> 00:04:04.000
The second component is the cognitive component where you're thinking about work all the time. And this is often not talked about enough, in my opinion, because it's this like running dialogue about work in your head. It keeps you up, keeps you from falling asleep. It distracts you when you're talking with your family and what are you doing? You're thinking about work. So that's the cognitive component. There's an emotional piece to it that goes along with introjected motivation, this feeling of guilt and anxiety that kind of builds and builds if you're not working, which is partly what

7
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:38.000
kind of drives you to pick up the phone or check your email. And then the fourth component is the most obvious. It's the behavioral component. Workaholics do tend to work longer than expected, longer than their colleagues. It is related to work hours, but it's honestly just kind of a mom. relationship or correlation. So doing things like working on vacation, all of that is part of the behavioral component. So it is truly multifaceted. You can think about it sort of like a compulsion to work using that loosely and not like in the technical clinical psychology sense.

8
00:04:38.000 --> 00:05:51.000
Now you've studied how workaholism affects people's lives, their physical health, their mental health, and their relationships. What have you and other researchers in this area found? Right. So pretty universally negative outcomes for individuals, for those around them, family, both in terms of physical health outcomes, we find that workaholism is linked to poor health. You know, they tend to not exercise as much sometimes or sleep very well. And that kind of takes a toll. But there's some recent research showing links with cardiovascular risk. and actograph studies like showing the sleep impacts of workaholism and even linked to inflammation. And so we see these health impacts. We see self-reported lower mental health, greater burnout, just generally in a kind of self-report of how you're feeling tends to be universally negative. And impacts on the family, I personally conducted interviews of spouses of workaholics. and we really see the impacts of the family by talking to the spouses.

9
00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:14.000
You know, the relationship quality suffers. The spouse is taking more on. So they're becoming more exhausted. They probably are becoming resentful. There's impacts on the children. And there's impacts at work. There's not enough research on this particular area, in my opinion. But we do see research on, you know, if a supervisor, for example, is higher in

10
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:55.000
workaholism, they are having negative impacts on their employees, that their employees are basically feeling like they need to be responding all the time because of this pressure that their boss is putting on them. And, you know, just in general, we find a workaholism related to negative workplace behaviors like counterproductive work behaviors, which we don't want at work. So things that indirectly or directly harm the organization. So this could be things like being rude to a coworker or those types of counterproductive work behaviors, not necessarily things like stealing and whatnot.

11
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:37.000
But yeah, we just see universally negative impacts on individuals, those around them, and their work suffers too. We are not finding relationships with job performance, either the self-reported or supervisor-reported performance that's consistent across all of our research, and we've conducted meta-analyses, which is basically a quantitative synthesis of all the research in this particular area, and we're not finding relationships with work performance. So workaholic behaviors are not making people better workers, and they're making them more difficult to be around, to be quite honest. In my opinion, it's very clear that the impact.

12
00:07:37.000 --> 00:08:43.000
are negative. Do workaholics themselves tend to think that by being on all the time that that's going to help their careers, even though the research shows that it doesn't? I mean, are they deluded? Well, for my book, I interviewed many individuals from workaholics Anonymous. So that's actually a real organization that exists. And so I feel like the answer to your question, it comes from, like, just hearing from self-described workaholics. And I can say that in many cases, yes, it's almost like they trick themselves into this is what I have to do, this is what I need, even in the face of negative health consequences. And I kid you not, every single person I interviewed from Workaholics Anonymous had some sort of health impact of their workaholism. And even with that, you know, I distinctly remember one man I spoke to, he said he will never stop workaholic. He will literally work until he dies. And it did not matter that he's had, you know, a heart attack in the past.

13
00:08:43.000 --> 00:09:15.000
And his wife has said he was, that she was going to divorce him. It didn't matter to him that was what he wanted. And with him, the only reason he was part of workaholics Anonymous is his wife said, you're going to do this or I'm leaving you. And so it was almost like, he's dragged into this. And I relate personally to, you know, this is why I studied it in the first place, is I relate to this idea of kind of telling yourself, I'm doing this for this reason, I'm doing this because I need to, you know, this will help me succeed. So I do think there is a

14
00:09:15.000 --> 00:10:02.000
lot of confirming this with yourself. Then are there particular jobs where the expectations are so high that workaholism is encouraged, if not expected? I mean, I'm thinking of some law firms where people are just expected to put in crazy hours. Or even, you know, here in D.C., people who work on Capitol Hill, they do crazy hours. I mean, you're expected to be there, you know, all night because Congress is in. You know, is that the problem that the jobs are encouraging workaholism? That's part of the problem. And, you know, there are certainly these types of jobs and occupations that the culture is, it's a workaholic culture. This is what newcomers are told when they get in. Let me give you an example. One woman I interviewed for the book, she

15
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:30.000
worked at one of the big consulting firms. And the very first day, her co-worker said, look, let me tell you something, if you ever need to leave to go to a doctor's appointment or something like that, leave your purse on your desk and just take your keys. Hmm, why would that be? Well, I mean, because we know that if we're going to actually leave work to go to an appointment, of course, we're going to take our purse. And so it was, you know, basically sending that signal that you don't leave your desk.

16
00:10:30.000 --> 00:11:14.000
And, you know, jobs like that, like you say. said in DC, some of the government jobs, I mean, doctors, lawyers, you name it. But what was really fascinating was in my research, you know, I have had people in all sorts of jobs, like across the whole range, K through 12 teachers. You know, I even had a race car driver and a llama farmer. I mean, it goes the whole range. So it's not linked to specific jobs. Another person I spoke with, she said she thought it was her job. And so she quit and she got a new job and realized she was still doing the same thing, even though that job wasn't as demanding with their organizational culture.

17
00:11:14.000 --> 00:12:02.000
And then that's when she realized, oh, this is partially internally driven. So the way I see it, there are some internal drivers that, you know, whether it's dispositional tendencies or whatnot that do play a role for sure. but the setting, the organizational culture, can exacerbate those workaholic tendencies, basically. Is it something that is typical of, say, perfectionists? Are those the kinds of people who become workaholics? They're highly related, I will say, yeah. So there's different kinds of perfectionists, too. And we see some of the more, I would say, maladaptive qualities of workaholics, such as feeling negative emotions when you're not working or thinking about work all the time,

18
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:41.000
In my research, those two in particular seem to be related to the aspect of perfectionism that is that you're never going to live up to your standards, your internal standards, whereas some people call it the more productive dimension of perfectionism, perfectionistic strivings, which is like perhaps considered the healthier aspect, that's more related to the behavioral and the motivational components of workaholism. But bottom line is they're very highly related. and we see perfectionistic tendencies in your childhood that are kind of like early indicators. So, yeah, a lot of workaholics are also perfectionists.

