00:12:41.000 --> 00:13:24.000
And just to follow up on that, I mean, if I were a parent of a child and I thought that there was potential workaholism flowering there, I mean, what would I be looking for? There is actually a concept called school addiction that I've read about. I haven't personally studied it. But yeah, you can, I think, look for the same sort of patterns, you know, if your child is constantly worried about their grade or you see them, you know, instead of taking a moment to play with friends, they are studying for their exam, which is two weeks away for hours, you know, and not being able to put their schoolwork down.

20
00:13:24.000 --> 00:14:17.000
I think those are really good indicators that there's some workaholic tendencies developing for sure. Well, our country is sometimes stereotyped as a country of workaholics, and I'm wondering if that's borne out in the research. There are some cross-cultural studies that show that some cultures, you know, on average, tend to show higher levels of workaholism. U.S. is one of them. Japan, China, South Korea, those are probably the ones that show the highest levels. What's interesting, though, is Western Europe countries, a lot of them, especially like the Nordic countries, we see lower levels on average again, but there's not a ton of research on this. But if I had to explain where most work-allism researchers are, it's in Europe, which we would think we need more of the research here,

21
00:14:17.000 --> 00:15:04.000
but that I think just goes to show that it's just culturally acceptable here. And, you know, maybe that's why in Europe we see more researchers studying this phenomenon because people take it more seriously. Well, we don't get a lot of vacation in this country, you know, compared to other places. And I'm wondering, is that something that would help mitigate workaholism or, you know, help to promote a healthier relationship that workers have with their workplace? I would love more vacation. Yeah. But would you take it? Oh, I mean, isn't that the million dollar question? Yeah. So I do think, you know, as a society, we need more sanctioned vacation days, you know, but regulations that, you know, mandate that organizations have to give so much vacation time

22
00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:54.000
because we are far short compared to other countries in terms of days off. But I do think that even with that, we would need to make sure people take it. You know, user-lose-it policies sometimes are beneficial. I think people don't want to lose something that they have, but things like unlimited vacation, time I don't really think are the solution because then it turns into this weird pressure of like, well, what are my peers taking? Like, what are my coworkers taking in terms of days off? I don't want to look, you know, like I'm taking too much. And so I definitely think that, you know, having it taken out of the workers control almost and, you know, the organization or some regulation saying, nope,

23
00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:42.000
these are the days you have to take. And, you know, there are even right to disconnect laws in some European countries. I don't know if I'm necessarily 100% on board with that, but it's an interesting concept. Right to Disconnect Laws is basically that the organization is not allowed to reach out to the employee if they are, you know, off the clock. So again, I don't know if I'm completely on board with that, but the philosophy behind that I am because I do think setting those norms and, you know, making sure it's not expected. And organizations really, really should, unless there's an emergency, that can help people to truly disconnect. We're going to take a short break. When we return, we'll talk about the technology that keeps us tethered to our work, even when we're

