27
00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:51.000
you know, being available all the time meant this person was up all night, basically. And so those are little signals that give indications of what is valued in that culture or in the organizational culture. And so what is rewarded, who is rewarded and why, but also things that the leader, I think the leader is, you know, the supervisor manager is the most direct impact on us. You know, if our boss is constantly texting us and emailing us and we know that their personality is such that they want a response immediately. A lot of workaholics have this tendency to think everything is urgent. And so if the manager or the boss has these workaholic tendencies and is constantly pinging their employees, and wanting an immediate response, that is going to affect the employees negatively.

28
00:19:51.000 --> 00:20:14.000
Regardless if they have workaholic tendencies, they're having to jump when the boss says jump. Well, what if you see these tendencies in yourself? What are some of the things you can do to reduce your workaholism? I mean, you said that that was an issue for you early in your career. So how do you manage this? It's a constant battle. I'm not going to lie. I mean, it's not like the tendencies go away, right?

29
00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:32.000
So I think just being really intentional and there's a variety of different strategies that I talk about in my book and it's not a one-size-fits-all so I think it's a matter of trial and error and see what works for you and I use a combination of things. You know, one strategy is

30
00:20:32.000 --> 00:21:07.000
scheduling myself into my daily to-do list like putting it on my calendar. Here's the time where I'm going to actually take a lunch break and not be on my computer, but just like relax and eat. Or like, send an alarm on my phone, okay, now it's time to walk around my building for, you know, five, ten minutes to just get some fresh air and get away from my desk. Those little moments of reprieve, like those are micro breaks and they can be really helpful

31
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:49.000
in recovery. So another thing that I do, there's a whole literature on recovery. strategies that's very related to, you know, the workaholism literature, obviously. And one recovery strategy from that literature is to engage in what's called a mastery experience. And I personally love this one. It works for me because things like relaxation and meditation, I have tried and I have failed. And I have tried again and I've failed. Something about just, being idle is very uncomfortable for me. And I don't love it.

32
00:21:49.000 --> 00:22:20.000
And so a mastery experience is a recovery strategy where you're immersing yourself into something not work-related, and it takes your attention and concentration. What I love about that is, one, it's been shown to be a very effective recovery strategy. So you feel more engaged the next day at work. you feel more rested and you're gaining back that energy that you lost, you know, throughout the whole day working.

33
00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:55.000
But I'm not sitting there idle. I'm doing something. You know, as long as it's something non-work related, I am psychologically detaching because I'm forcing myself basically to think about something else. So this could be learning a new language. You know, my editor for the book, he learned, you know, to play, he taught himself how to play guitar, you know, so really anything that you get joy out of that you can focus on is, is my favorite recovery strategy for workaholics, because it really does seem that a lot of people

